<!-- Build template. Terminology: application programming interface (API); command-line interface (CLI); Python Package Index (PyPI); Verifier Standard (VSTD). -->
<div class="home-intro"><div class="eyebrow">VERIFIER STANDARD · DEVELOPER DOCUMENTATION</div>
<h1>Make computational claims<br><em>checkable.</em></h1>
<p class="home-lead">Capture a computation. Bind its artifacts to a precise claim. Give someone else a bounded way to inspect, reproduce, or refute it.</p>
<p class="home-description">Verifier is the Python reference implementation of the Verifier Standard (VSTD), a verification-domain language for portable, bounded, refutable evidence about computational claims.</p>
<div class="home-actions"><a class="portal-button primary" href="@/docs/INSTALLATION.html">Start with Verifier <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="portal-button" href="@/{{release_path}}reference.html#api">Explore the Python reference</a></div></div>
<section id="start-building" class="start-panel"><div><span class="eyebrow">FROM PACKAGE TO FIRST CHECK</span><h2>A small place to start.</h2><p>Install from the Python Package Index (PyPI). Run the defensive demo before capturing your own computation.</p><a href="@/docs/FIRST_RECEIPT.html">Follow the receipt walkthrough <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div><div class="terminal"><div class="terminal-top"><span class="terminal-dots" aria-hidden="true">● ● ●</span> Terminal <span>Python 3.10+</span></div><pre><code>python -m pip install verifier-standard=={{release}}
vstd demo
vstd --help</code></pre><div class="terminal-foot">Distribution: verifier-standard · Import: verifier · Command: vstd</div></div></section>
<section id="explore" class="explore-section"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Find your path</h2><span>Learn → inspect → contribute</span></div><div class="portal-cards">
<a class="portal-card" href="@/docs/CONCEPTS_AND_PRECEDENTS.html"><span class="card-icon" aria-hidden="true">01</span><h3>Understand the model <span>↗</span></h3><p>Claims, evidence, coordinates, and the limits that give a result its meaning.</p><small>CONCEPTS & PRECEDENTS</small></a>
<a class="portal-card" href="@/{{release_path}}reference.html#cli"><span class="card-icon" aria-hidden="true">02</span><h3>Use the package <span>↗</span></h3><p>Generated command-line interface (CLI) and Python application programming interface (API) references, pinned to release {{release}}.</p><small>RELEASED REFERENCE</small></a>
<a class="portal-card" href="@/docs/REPOSITORY_WALKTHROUGH.html"><span class="card-icon" aria-hidden="true">03</span><h3>Explore the repository <span>↗</span></h3><p>Trace a command into its implementation, examples, tests, and normative source.</p><small>REPOSITORY WALKTHROUGH</small></a>
<a class="portal-card" href="@/project/CONTRIBUTING.html"><span class="card-icon" aria-hidden="true">04</span><h3>Make a contribution <span>↗</span></h3><p>Bring a counterexample, improve a guide, or tighten a declared verification boundary.</p><small>CONTRIBUTOR GUIDE</small></a></div></section>
<section id="claim-boundaries" class="boundary-panel"><span class="boundary-symbol" aria-hidden="true">◇</span><div><h2>Know what a result means.</h2><p>A successful check supports its named proposition under its declared conditions. It does not establish every surrounding claim. Missing evidence stays <code>UNKNOWN</code>.</p><a href="@/docs/CLAIMS_AND_LIMITS.html">Read the claims and limits →</a></div></section>
